                <pb facs="0620" n="486" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg486"/>

                <head>No. 340.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Anderson to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-02-27"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 27, 1888</date>.
                        (Received March 16.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 210.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that I was
                    informed day before yesterday that the committee of the upper house of the
                    Rigsdag, to which the petroleum bill introduced by the minister of justice has
                    been referred, will doubtless report an amendment to the bill in favor of
                    raising the fire test of petroleum to 40° Celsius, a test equal to that of what
                    is known in America as “water-white” oil.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. B.
                            Anderson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
